'''Nèstir Cavall Diop''' (born 17 June 2001) is a Balboan professional {{w|Association football|footballer}} who plays as a {{w|Midfielder|central midfielder}} for [[Castejón FC]] in the [[Baliscan Premier League]] and the [[Balboa national football team]]. The son of [[Nèstir Cavall]], widely regarded as the greatest Balboan footballer of all time, Cavall Jr. has established himself as a key player for both club and country in his own right, though his playing style and position differ significantly from his father's.

== Early life ==

Cavall was born in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], Balboa, the eldest child of [[Nèstir Cavall]] and Amara Diop. His father was at the peak of his career during Cavall's childhood — the 2006 AFA World Cup triumph occurred when Cavall Jr. was five years old, and the family relocated to [[Castejón]], [[Balisca]], in 2004 when his father signed for Castejón FC.{{sfn|Diop|2015|p=42}}

Cavall spent his formative years in Castejón, where he was immersed in the Baliscan football system. He joined the [[Castejón FC]] youth academy at the age of nine and progressed through the age groups alongside the children of other Baliscan and expatriate players.{{sfn|Diop|2015|p=44}} The family returned to Balboa during school holidays, and Cavall maintained his connection to Sant Cristòfor through his mother's family and his aunt, [[Bintu Cavall]], who was still playing for [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] at the time.{{sfn|Diop|2015|p=45}}

Despite his father's fame, Cavall has stated that his childhood was relatively normal: "In Castejón, Dad was just another player. At school, I was Nèstir's son, but on the pitch, I was just another kid. That was healthy."{{sfn|Diop|2015|p=46}}

== Club career ==

=== FC Sant Cristòfor (2019–2022) ===

In 2018, at the age of seventeen, Cavall made the decision to leave the Castejón youth system and return to Balboa, joining the [[FC Sant Cristòfor]] youth setup.{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=12}} The move surprised some observers, who expected him to remain in the Baliscan system, but Cavall cited a desire to establish himself independently of his father's legacy at Castejón: "I needed to prove I could do it without the name carrying me. Sant Cristòfor was where Dad started, but it was also where I could be myself."{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=13}}

Cavall was promoted to the FC Sant Cristòfor first team for the 2019 Apertura. He made his La Lliga debut on 16 February 2019, starting in a 1–1 draw against [[Atlètic Costabella]]. Over three seasons, he made 52 appearances and scored 6 goals, establishing himself as a composed, technically gifted central midfielder with a strong passing range and defensive awareness.{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=18}}

=== Castejón FC (2022–present) ===

In 2022, Cavall returned to Castejón FC, signing a five-year contract with the club where his father had spent his peak years.{{sfn|Herrera|2022}} The transfer was widely discussed in the Balboan and Baliscan media, with some commentators questioning whether Cavall Jr. had earned the move on merit or whether the Cavall name had influenced the decision.{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=24}}

Cavall silenced the doubters with a strong first season, making 28 league appearances and scoring 4 goals as Castejón finished second in the Baliscan Premier League. His performances earned him a regular starting place in the 2023–24 season, and he has since become an integral part of the Castejón midfield.{{sfn|Herrera|2024}}

His playing style at Castejón has been characterised by tactical intelligence, ball retention, and an ability to dictate the tempo of matches from deep positions — a role that contrasts sharply with his father's explosive attacking play.{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=30}} Castejón manager [[Carlos Mendez]] described Cavall Jr. as "the metronome of our midfield — he doesn't score the goals his father scored, but he makes the team function."{{sfn|Herrera|2024}}

== International career ==

Cavall made his debut for the [[Balboa national football team]] in March 2021, at the age of nineteen, in a [[2022 AFA World Cup]] qualifier against [[Dagit]].{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=22}} He scored his first international goal in his fourth appearance, a long-range strike in a 3–0 victory over [[Sarta]].

He was included in Balboa's squad for the [[2022 AFA World Cup]], where he made two substitute appearances.{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=26}} By the [[2026 AFA World Cup]] qualifying campaign, he had established himself as a regular starter, forming a midfield partnership with [[Yassine Brifà]].

Cavall was named to Balboa's 26-player squad for the [[2026 AFA World Cup]].{{sfn|Mifà|2026}} As of June 2026, he has earned 29 caps and scored 5 goals for Balboa.

== Personal life ==

Cavall is the son of [[Nèstir Cavall]] and Amara Diop. His younger sister is Kèsim Cavall (born 2008). His aunt is [[Bintu Cavall]], head coach of the [[Balboa women's national football team]], and his cousin [[Liang Cavall]] plays as a forward for [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] and the Balboa national team.{{sfn|Diop|2015|p=7}}

Cavall has been in a relationship with Baliscan journalist Elena Ruiz since 2023. He divides his time between Castejón and Sant Cristòfor during the off-season.{{sfn|Herrera|2024}}

He has been open about the pressure of carrying the Cavall name: "People expect me to be my father. I'm not — I'm a different player, a different person. The name is a privilege and a burden, but I wouldn't change it."{{sfn|Brifà|2023|p=32}}

